# Java Comparable vs Comparator

This repository explains the difference between **Comparable** and **Comparator** in Java and how they are used to sort custom objects.

---

## 📌 The Problem

Java can easily sort primitive values and strings.

```java
List<Integer> numbers = Arrays.asList(30, 10, 20);

Collections.sort(numbers);

System.out.println(numbers);
```

Output:

```text
[10, 20, 30]
```

But what happens when we want to sort our own objects?

For example:

```java
Student s1 = new Student("Rahul", 85);
Student s2 = new Student("Ananya", 72);
Student s3 = new Student("Zoya", 95);
```

How does Java know which student should come first?

This is where **Comparable** and **Comparator** are used.

---

# 1️⃣ Comparable

`Comparable` is used to define the **natural ordering** of objects.

A class implements:

```java
Comparable<T>
```

and overrides:

```java
compareTo()
```

### Example: Sorting Students by Marks

```java
import java.util.*;

class Student implements Comparable<Student> {

    String name;
    int marks;

    Student(String name, int marks) {
        this.name = name;
        this.marks = marks;
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Student other) {
        return Integer.compare(this.marks, other.marks);
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return name + " - " + marks;
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ArrayList<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();

        students.add(new Student("Rahul", 85));
        students.add(new Student("Ananya", 72));
        students.add(new Student("Zoya", 95));

        Collections.sort(students);

        System.out.println(students);
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
[Ananya - 72, Rahul - 85, Zoya - 95]
```

Here, the `Student` class defines its own default sorting order based on marks.

---

## 🔍 How `compareTo()` Works

`compareTo()` returns an integer.

```text
Negative value → Current object comes before the other object

Zero → Both objects are equal in ordering

Positive value → Current object comes after the other object
```

Example:

```java
return Integer.compare(this.marks, other.marks);
```

---

# 2️⃣ Comparator

`Comparator` is used when we want **custom sorting**.

For example, the same students can be sorted by:

* Name
* Marks
* Age
* ID

Unlike `Comparable`, we don't need to define the sorting logic permanently inside the class.

---

## Example: Sorting Students by Name

```java
import java.util.*;

class Student {

    String name;
    int marks;

    Student(String name, int marks) {
        this.name = name;
        this.marks = marks;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return name + " - " + marks;
    }
}

public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ArrayList<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();

        students.add(new Student("Rahul", 85));
        students.add(new Student("Ananya", 72));
        students.add(new Student("Zoya", 95));

        Comparator<Student> byName =
                (s1, s2) -> s1.name.compareTo(s2.name);

        students.sort(byName);

        System.out.println(students);
    }
}
```

### Output

```text
[Ananya - 72, Rahul - 85, Zoya - 95]
```

---

# 3️⃣ Multiple Ways to Sort

This is where `Comparator` becomes powerful.

### Sort by Name

```java
Comparator<Student> byName =
        Comparator.comparing(s -> s.name);
```

### Sort by Marks

```java
Comparator<Student> byMarks =
        Comparator.comparingInt(s -> s.marks);
```

Now we can choose how to sort:

```java
students.sort(byName);
```

or:

```java
students.sort(byMarks);
```

---

# ⬇️ Descending Order

We can reverse the sorting order using:

```java
students.sort(byMarks.reversed());
```

For example:

```text
Ascending Order:
72 → 85 → 95

Descending Order:
95 → 85 → 72
```

---

# 🔗 Sorting by Multiple Fields

Suppose two students have the same marks.

We can first sort by marks and then by name.

```java
Comparator<Student> studentComparator =
        Comparator
                .comparingInt((Student s) -> s.marks)
                .thenComparing(s -> s.name);
```

Java will:

```text
Step 1 → Compare marks

Step 2 → If marks are equal, compare names
```

---

# ⚖️ Comparable vs Comparator

| Comparable                            | Comparator                           |
| ------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Defines natural ordering              | Defines custom ordering              |
| Uses `compareTo()`                    | Uses `compare()`                     |
| Implemented inside the class          | Usually created outside the class    |
| Usually provides one default ordering | Can provide multiple sorting methods |
| Uses `Comparable<T>`                  | Uses `Comparator<T>`                 |

---

# 🧠 Easy Way to Remember

```text
Comparable
↓
"What is the default way to sort my objects?"

Comparator
↓
"How do I want to sort these objects right now?"
```

---

# 🌍 Real-World Example

Imagine an e-commerce application with products.

Each product has:

```text
Name
Price
Rating
```

Users may want to sort products differently:

```text
💰 Price: Low → High

⭐ Rating: High → Low

🔤 Name: A → Z
```

Using `Comparator`, we can create different sorting strategies.

```java
Comparator<Product> byPrice =
        Comparator.comparingDouble(p -> p.price);

Comparator<Product> byRating =
        Comparator.comparingDouble(p -> p.rating)
                  .reversed();

Comparator<Product> byName =
        Comparator.comparing(p -> p.name);
```

This allows the same objects to be sorted in multiple ways.

---

# 💡 Key Takeaway

```text
Comparable
→ Natural/default ordering
→ compareTo()
→ Usually one sorting strategy

Comparator
→ Custom ordering
→ compare()
→ Multiple sorting strategies
```

Both are important when working with:

* `Collections.sort()`
* `List.sort()`
* `TreeSet`
* `TreeMap`
* Custom Objects
* Real-world sorting requirements

---

### ⭐ Conclusion

Use **Comparable** when your class has a natural default order.

Use **Comparator** when you need different ways to sort the same objects.

Understanding both concepts is important for working with **Java Collections and custom objects**.
